VALUES ('906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 'ccs-practice-5', 'CCS Practice Paper 5', 'ccs', ARRAY['ICD-10-CM', 'ICD-10-PCS', 'Nephrology/Dialysis', 'Pulmonary Procedures', 'GI Endoscopy', 'Wound Care', 'EMTALA', 'Accreditation', 'Telehealth']::TEXT[], 100, 'advanced', true, 5, 240);

-- ── Section: Domain 1 — Coding Knowledge and Skills (40 questions) — Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA's CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank. ──

VALUES ('1a5a2e56-1516-43fd-87dc-8fe04536fb32', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 1, 'A patient with ESRD undergoes creation of an arteriovenous (AV) fistula for future hemodialysis access. Which ICD-10-PCS root operation applies to creating this new connection between an artery and vein?');
VALUES ('1a5a2e56-1516-43fd-87dc-8fe04536fb32', '579f2f8d-aaaa-4fc4-a794-ea2075376210', 'Creating an AV fistula establishes a new route for blood flow between an artery and vein — mapping to Bypass, consistent with the same root operation used for CABG (routing blood through an alternate pathway).', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('f16611d4-7c6a-4299-af87-709901571ab3', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 2, 'A patient receives hemodialysis during an inpatient stay for an unrelated acute condition. Which of the following best reflects correct coding of the dialysis sessions performed during this stay?');
VALUES ('f16611d4-7c6a-4299-af87-709901571ab3', '114fea20-bc45-4b63-acb7-5a3f843a6190', 'Hemodialysis procedures actually performed during a stay are coded (via the appropriate ICD-10-PCS section covering extracorporeal or performance procedures) in addition to the admission''s principal diagnosis and the ESRD diagnosis itself — reinforcing the general principle that all procedures actually performed during a stay are coded.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('7cdf3052-9a48-43f7-a074-409d2dca3a62', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 3, 'A patient undergoes a peritoneal dialysis catheter placement. Which ICD-10-PCS root operation most likely applies to placing this catheter?');
VALUES ('7cdf3052-9a48-43f7-a074-409d2dca3a62', '7284bfb8-6b28-4a53-8372-9cd84a7ca34d', 'A peritoneal dialysis catheter is a device placed to assist/perform a physiological function (fluid exchange) without physically replacing a body part — mapping to Insertion, consistent with other device-placement scenarios (e.g., pacemaker leads) already discussed.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('b4de3e14-e310-405b-9a73-c01080171945', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 4, 'Which of the following best describes correct ICD-10-CM coding when a patient''s CKD is directly attributable to long-standing type 2 diabetes, as documented by the physician?');
VALUES ('b4de3e14-e310-405b-9a73-c01080171945', '7a0da4eb-13a9-4ded-9a21-41f354221bc5', 'This reinforces the earlier diabetes-CKD combination coding principle (presumed causal relationship per classification) with the additional specific-stage coding requirement — a recurring, heavily tested diabetes-complication coding pattern.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('252fb9c1-5fb8-4b60-a585-56b7b6b3925f', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 5, 'A patient undergoes a bronchoscopy with bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL) for diagnostic evaluation of a pulmonary infiltrate. Which ICD-10-PCS root operation most likely applies to the lavage/fluid collection portion of this procedure?');
VALUES ('252fb9c1-5fb8-4b60-a585-56b7b6b3925f', 'aefd0bbe-fd7b-4160-b08e-c46e7fa820ff', 'BAL involves instilling and then withdrawing fluid to sample the lower respiratory tract — the fluid-removal portion maps to Drainage, appropriately qualified as diagnostic when performed for diagnostic sampling purposes rather than therapeutic fluid removal.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('cbbe48a4-be09-435e-806a-7641c3484eaf', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 6, 'A patient undergoes therapeutic bronchoscopy with foreign body removal from the airway. Which ICD-10-PCS root operation most likely applies?');
VALUES ('cbbe48a4-be09-435e-806a-7641c3484eaf', '9ac35e23-69bc-4049-b096-9714a2099967', 'Removing a foreign body maps to either Extraction or Extirpation depending on the specific mechanism/nature of the material (mirroring the same distinction already tested for other solid-matter removal scenarios like D&C vs. thrombus removal) — not Insertion, Replacement, or Supplement, which don''t reflect a removal action.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('e560f0f6-2366-4069-9df7-fbf696327dfa', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 7, 'A patient undergoes an esophagogastroduodenoscopy (EGD) with biopsy of a gastric ulcer. Which of the following best describes correct coding for the biopsy portion of this procedure?');
VALUES ('e560f0f6-2366-4069-9df7-fbf696327dfa', '8cf3f23f-e74f-463b-92c2-a1bedbe221f9', 'A biopsy taking a portion (not all) of tissue for diagnostic purposes maps to Excision with the diagnostic qualifier — a frequently tested, foundational ICD-10-PCS biopsy-coding principle applicable across many body systems.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('23cad882-023e-4b1c-9802-77374989a2dd', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 8, 'A patient undergoes a colonoscopy with polypectomy via snare technique. Which of the following best describes correct coding for the polyp removal?');
VALUES ('23cad882-023e-4b1c-9802-77374989a2dd', '1c5dce83-10e8-4ecc-9e0d-9ad3bbdf2a09', 'A snare polypectomy removes a portion of tissue (the polyp and its base) — mapping to Excision, not Resection (which would require removing the entire organ, not applicable here) — reinforcing the Excision-versus-Resection distinction in yet another common GI procedure context.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('b6138108-9fb3-4030-a5df-7900f78d098a', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 9, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for a colonoscopy during which a polyp is removed via hot biopsy forceps using electrocautery to destroy tissue at the base, with no portion of tissue physically extracted for pathology?');
VALUES ('b6138108-9fb3-4030-a5df-7900f78d098a', '585c1486-386b-44e2-b4e7-d24e3b7b323c', 'This distinguishes a genuinely destructive-only technique (no tissue removed, purely destroyed in place) from Excision (tissue physically removed) — testing whether the specific technique documented (not just ''polyp removal'' generically) correctly determines root operation selection.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('8fb3f20a-7f83-4390-9e41-01c2c7279998', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 10, 'A patient with a documented non-pressure chronic ulcer of the lower leg due to peripheral vascular disease should be coded using:');
VALUES ('8fb3f20a-7f83-4390-9e41-01c2c7279998', 'e30254a9-72b3-423a-8f0e-7f0cf314c8f0', 'Non-pressure chronic ulcers (e.g., venous stasis, arterial insufficiency ulcers) occupy their own distinct ICD-10-CM code category — structurally similar in requiring site/laterality/depth specificity to pressure ulcer codes, but a genuinely separate category reflecting a different etiology.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('fbc37469-79eb-4245-9803-5f30f8276e48', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 11, 'Which of the following best describes correct ICD-10-CM coding for a diabetic foot ulcer, specifically regarding the combination-coding and additional-specificity principles involved?');
VALUES ('fbc37469-79eb-4245-9803-5f30f8276e48', 'dcaae6f1-e696-4c30-87ff-ba7aa230a2a9', 'Diabetic foot ulcer coding requires BOTH the diabetes-ulcer combination code AND an additional code specifying the ulcer''s exact site/laterality/depth from the non-pressure ulcer category — a two-code requirement frequently missed by coders who stop at the combination code alone.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('8d99007c-c1e7-4c3c-a330-57034c78ce26', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 12, 'A patient undergoes debridement of a diabetic foot ulcer down to and including subcutaneous tissue, performed via sharp excisional technique. Which of the following best describes correct ICD-10-PCS coding?');
VALUES ('8d99007c-c1e7-4c3c-a330-57034c78ce26', '4c6d65a7-0358-49c1-9a3a-5d35860569bb', 'This reapplies the depth-dependent excisional debridement principle (already covered for a pressure ulcer scenario) to a diabetic foot ulcer — the deepest tissue layer actually debrided (here, subcutaneous tissue) determines the body part value used with the Excision root operation.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('4a53205f-393b-4df5-a7e8-854b7604d431', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 13, 'A patient with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease undergoes a procedure to reduce hyperinflated lung volume by removing diseased lung tissue (lung volume reduction surgery). Which ICD-10-PCS root operation most likely applies?');
VALUES ('4a53205f-393b-4df5-a7e8-854b7604d431', 'baa138fa-539e-4ded-9797-882a82114411', 'Lung volume reduction surgery removes diseased lung tissue — whether it maps to Excision (a portion) or Resection (an entire lobe) depends on the specific extent documented, applying the same fundamental portion-versus-entire principle used throughout ICD-10-PCS.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('a4e5cae7-0e2a-4252-8ba3-6d1d6c703cd9', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 14, 'A patient undergoes tracheostomy placement for prolonged mechanical ventilation. Which ICD-10-PCS root operation most likely applies to creating the tracheostomy opening itself?');
VALUES ('a4e5cae7-0e2a-4252-8ba3-6d1d6c703cd9', '6de6fb8d-b7e7-4c5a-acb5-9052a7278150', 'Tracheostomy is generally classified under Bypass, reflecting its function of creating an alternate route for airflow bypassing the natural upper airway — consistent with Bypass''s core definition of altering the route of passage of a tubular body part''s contents (here, air).', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('a7432efc-0c80-40f6-be90-5e9d3401484a', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 15, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for a patient who requires prolonged mechanical ventilation during an inpatient stay, with respect to the ventilator hours/duration documentation?');
VALUES ('a7432efc-0c80-40f6-be90-5e9d3401484a', '5bc3f1d0-244c-42d4-9727-49737c46efaa', 'Mechanical ventilation ICD-10-PCS codes are duration-banded, making accurate start/stop time documentation a direct code-selection factor — a frequently tested, practically significant respiratory-procedure coding detail with real DRG implications.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('7f83c30f-dd4e-4949-bfec-3cdeaffe0e89', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 16, 'A patient''s ventilator is initiated in the Emergency Department at 2:00 PM and the patient is subsequently admitted as an inpatient at 6:00 PM the same day, with ventilation continuing throughout. For calculating total ventilator hours for ICD-10-PCS coding purposes on the inpatient stay, which of the following is most accurate?');
VALUES ('7f83c30f-dd4e-4949-bfec-3cdeaffe0e89', 'bec2bf1b-3f20-4cac-a64c-ecae92140958', 'Ventilator-hour counting guidance directs capturing the actual duration of ventilatory support, including time from a directly-converting ED encounter, rather than artificially starting the clock only at the formal inpatient admission order — a specific, frequently tested nuance of ventilator-hour calculation.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('3eb8500d-6fb8-4bf8-873e-74ee8344549f', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 17, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for a patient who is intubated and placed on a ventilator, but dies before completing 24 hours of ventilation, with the death itself being the reason ventilation stopped (not weaning)?');
VALUES ('3eb8500d-6fb8-4bf8-873e-74ee8344549f', '0a78d9b3-1c69-483c-91b2-1c904c2dd243', 'The applicable duration-banded ventilator code reflects the ACTUAL hours of ventilatory support provided, regardless of why ventilation ultimately stopped (weaning, death, or other reasons) — outcome doesn''t change the coding of actual duration provided.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('699ec754-be3e-4847-a2fc-ab748f36aa98', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 18, 'A patient undergoes endoscopic retrograde cholangiopancreatography (ERCP) with removal of a common bile duct stone. Which of the following best describes correct ICD-10-PCS coding for the stone removal?');
VALUES ('699ec754-be3e-4847-a2fc-ab748f36aa98', 'cb8bcec6-a908-4dd4-bc16-ba17f67df0f0', 'A bile duct stone is solid matter with no natural/created channel to the outside — removing it maps to Extirpation, the same root operation concept already applied to thrombus and D&C-type scenarios, now applied to a biliary stone.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('ecbf3146-a8af-4ed9-94c5-0a35227820b0', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 19, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for a laparoscopic cholecystectomy converted to open due to a difficult dissection, ultimately completed via open technique with the entire gallbladder removed?');
VALUES ('ecbf3146-a8af-4ed9-94c5-0a35227820b0', '0aa0379e-458b-481c-8a97-ebb0c3598fed', 'This combines two previously-tested principles in one scenario: Resection is correct because the entire gallbladder (not a portion) is removed, and the approach reported is the one actually completed (open), not the initially attempted laparoscopic approach.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('1aacc497-8252-4b5d-87e3-9f0dd8462ff9', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 20, 'A patient with community-acquired pneumonia is found to have a specific causative organism (e.g., Streptococcus pneumoniae) identified via sputum culture, clearly documented by the physician as the causative organism. Correct coding requires:');
VALUES ('1aacc497-8252-4b5d-87e3-9f0dd8462ff9', 'f5c81aea-68ef-473b-bec4-9b93cba643c3', 'When a specific causative organism is clearly documented by the physician (supported by culture results), coding to that organism-specific level (via combination code or an additional organism code, per the specific classification structure) is expected — under-coding to unspecified when specificity is documented is the same recurring theme applied to a respiratory-infection scenario.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('ab15c223-c5b7-43e3-a6fe-d9915602a2ec', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 21, 'A patient with chronic kidney disease stage 5 (not yet on dialysis) is admitted for management of severe hyperkalemia. Which of the following best reflects correct principal diagnosis selection?');
VALUES ('ab15c223-c5b7-43e3-a6fe-d9915602a2ec', 'd829e964-f3fd-44c6-bac5-bdb8649eefe8', 'This reapplies the core UHDDS principal-diagnosis principle to a nephrology scenario — the acute condition chiefly responsible for THIS admission (hyperkalemia, or its acute cause) is the likely principal diagnosis, with the chronic CKD stage coded as a clinically significant additional diagnosis, not automatically overriding principal diagnosis selection.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('70287973-5052-4073-b613-1be3ff6438c8', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 22, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for a patient with ESRD who receives a kidney transplant during the current inpatient stay?');
VALUES ('70287973-5052-4073-b613-1be3ff6438c8', 'f1c43719-4703-46df-88bd-099ac5a5ced9', 'Kidney transplantation maps to the Transplantation root operation (a living or deceased-donor organ physically taking the place of/functioning for the failed kidney) — distinguishing it from Replacement, which is used for non-living/prosthetic substitutes, and noting that ESRD is still coded for the current admission, with post-transplant status becoming relevant for subsequent encounters.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('102acad6-c470-48d8-aee7-14eb710fcbe5', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 23, 'Which of the following best describes the coding relevance of documenting whether a kidney transplant is from a living-related, living-unrelated, or deceased donor?');
VALUES ('102acad6-c470-48d8-aee7-14eb710fcbe5', '82ffc7a7-10d9-4445-a940-780d6d3dc2a8', 'This reapplies the donor-source qualifier principle (already established for stem cell/bone marrow transplant) to solid organ transplantation — the documented donor-source relationship is a genuine, code-selection-relevant detail, not merely administrative/allocation-related information.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('42102ee9-38dd-49ad-b9ff-ada59fc5446a', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 24, 'A patient with acute respiratory failure requiring BiPAP (bilevel positive airway pressure, a form of noninvasive ventilation) rather than invasive mechanical ventilation should be coded using:');
VALUES ('42102ee9-38dd-49ad-b9ff-ada59fc5446a', 'f3199570-ae93-4495-815a-b934dac5da8b', 'Noninvasive ventilation (BiPAP/CPAP) is coded distinctly from invasive mechanical ventilation — a real, clinically and codably significant distinction, not an interchangeable substitute, reinforcing that the SPECIFIC type of respiratory support actually documented determines correct coding.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('6bc8cc98-fdbf-4b36-aa4c-32c730715f98', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 25, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for a patient who undergoes therapeutic thoracentesis for a large pleural effusion causing respiratory distress?');
VALUES ('6bc8cc98-fdbf-4b36-aa4c-32c730715f98', 'd8e4c2aa-69af-45de-a852-66f71bc7d784', 'Therapeutic thoracentesis removes fluid for symptomatic relief — mapping to Drainage with a therapeutic (not diagnostic) qualifier, reinforcing the diagnostic-versus-therapeutic qualifier distinction already established for other Drainage scenarios like BAL.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('b9695fef-b352-42dd-9369-0bb2ea44b658', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 26, 'A patient undergoes chest tube placement for pneumothorax. Which ICD-10-PCS root operation most likely applies to the chest tube (pleural drain) placement itself?');
VALUES ('b9695fef-b352-42dd-9369-0bb2ea44b658', 'd9187103-fb2d-46f6-a3be-e94f532b0a65', 'Chest tube placement for pneumothorax is coded as Drainage (taking out air/fluid from the pleural space), with the device value reflecting the drainage device (tube) left in place — Drainage, not Insertion, since the clinical PURPOSE is fluid/air removal, not placing a monitoring/assisting device per se.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('8bb19c20-b59e-42ff-bde8-a7d4516a44b3', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 27, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for a patient undergoing pleurodesis (a procedure to obliterate the pleural space and prevent recurrent pleural effusion/pneumothorax, often using a chemical or mechanical irritant)?');
VALUES ('8bb19c20-b59e-42ff-bde8-a7d4516a44b3', '597ce286-34b4-40d4-b3f7-690a8b9d3691', 'Pleurodesis''s goal (obliterating the pleural space via a chemical/mechanical irritant causing adhesion) aligns with Destruction''s definition of eradicating tissue/function via direct energy/chemical application — distinct from more common procedures like simple thoracentesis or chest tube placement alone.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('29b6e837-bf71-42fa-afe8-0e87a7bfbed7', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 28, 'Which of the following best describes correct ICD-10-CM coding for ''acute on chronic respiratory failure'' when both hypoxemic and hypercapnic components are documented?');
VALUES ('29b6e837-bf71-42fa-afe8-0e87a7bfbed7', 'bde51f20-2d17-4457-8614-bb375f407fcf', 'This reapplies the acute-on-chronic combination-coding principle (already established for heart failure) to respiratory failure, which similarly has acuity- and type-specific code options — under-coding to a generic or single-acuity respiratory failure code when more specific documentation exists misses this specificity.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('383aa33f-5ed2-440f-9a77-250f16c561b9', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 29, 'A patient undergoes a diagnostic upper GI endoscopy revealing Barrett''s esophagus with biopsy confirming low-grade dysplasia. Which of the following best reflects correct ICD-10-CM coding?');
VALUES ('383aa33f-5ed2-440f-9a77-250f16c561b9', '0072fbd5-7712-43ee-a821-6394eaf560bc', 'ICD-10-CM distinguishes Barrett''s esophagus without dysplasia from Barrett''s esophagus with dysplasia (and by grade, where provided) — using the non-dysplasia code when dysplasia has actually been confirmed and documented under-codes the clinical picture''s actual severity/surveillance implications.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('c2fc06ce-f9bc-4dd3-9bc9-6c233d73a7ae', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 30, 'Which of the following best describes correct ICD-10-PCS coding for endoscopic band ligation of esophageal varices (a technique to control/prevent variceal bleeding by placing rubber bands around the varices)?');
VALUES ('c2fc06ce-f9bc-4dd3-9bc9-6c233d73a7ae', 'd5d6b76e-124f-4105-bb79-b50dd5952759', 'Band ligation controls variceal bleeding by completely occluding blood flow to the varix (via the band) — mapping to Occlusion, distinct from Restriction (partial narrowing) or approaches that would involve actually removing tissue.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('3fc8ce96-1e36-4676-b1b7-f1128e06fa0c', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 31, 'A patient with cirrhosis and esophageal varices is admitted with acute variceal hemorrhage. Correct principal diagnosis selection would most likely be:');
VALUES ('3fc8ce96-1e36-4676-b1b7-f1128e06fa0c', '2fb89106-5a99-42c8-9868-2b5455aca330', 'The acute condition chiefly responsible for this specific admission (bleeding esophageal varices) is the likely principal diagnosis per UHDDS, with the underlying chronic cirrhosis coded as a clinically significant additional diagnosis — reinforcing the same UHDDS principal-diagnosis-selection logic applied across multiple body systems throughout this content.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('bd52d9f9-6e74-4a36-9e7d-a8b1b3ab569d', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 32, 'Which of the following best describes correct ICD-10-CM coding for hepatic encephalopathy occurring in a patient with underlying cirrhosis?');
VALUES ('bd52d9f9-6e74-4a36-9e7d-a8b1b3ab569d', '509136f4-ca0c-4d89-be4c-6b83f4ed4bb4', 'Hepatic encephalopathy has its own specific ICD-10-CM code, generally following the etiology/manifestation instructional-note convention (already covered generally in Domain 1) linking it back to the underlying liver disease — not a generic altered-mental-status code disconnected from the hepatic etiology.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('26b87353-2b10-4c6a-96c4-5cb073c3e415', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 33, 'A patient undergoes paracentesis for large-volume ascites related to cirrhosis, performed for symptomatic relief. Which ICD-10-PCS root operation and qualifier most likely apply?');
VALUES ('26b87353-2b10-4c6a-96c4-5cb073c3e415', 'b44a936a-30a1-4845-95fd-71470d6541fc', 'Therapeutic paracentesis for symptomatic ascites relief maps to Drainage with the therapeutic qualifier — the same Drainage-plus-qualifier structure already established for thoracentesis, applied here to the peritoneal cavity.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('4917bcb6-51fa-48c3-9c1f-2d534b047c9f', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 34, 'Which of the following best describes correct ICD-10-CM coding for spontaneous bacterial peritonitis (SBP) in a cirrhotic patient with ascites?');
VALUES ('4917bcb6-51fa-48c3-9c1f-2d534b047c9f', 'b950b32c-98c1-4ec6-9f94-749bdb34caf9', 'SBP is a distinct, specifically classified clinical entity (infected ascitic fluid without an evident surgical/perforation source) with its own ICD-10-CM code — distinguishing it from generic or surgical-source peritonitis codes reflects accurate, guideline-consistent hepatology coding.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('d835a454-ea0b-476e-a342-6c2c9665aaae', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 35, 'Which of the following best describes correct ICD-10-PCS coding for transjugular intrahepatic portosystemic shunt (TIPS) placement, a procedure creating a channel between the portal and hepatic veins to reduce portal hypertension?');
VALUES ('d835a454-ea0b-476e-a342-6c2c9665aaae', '14b40d93-1ac9-47aa-b7b1-98c7a8017797', 'TIPS creates an entirely new route for blood flow between two venous systems to relieve portal hypertension — mapping to Bypass, the same root operation concept already established for CABG and AV fistula creation, now applied to a hepatology/interventional-radiology context.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('6b50f3d1-5ac5-47dd-8289-be1c515bd644', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 36, 'A patient with alcohol-related liver disease and documented alcohol use disorder should generally be coded using:');
VALUES ('6b50f3d1-5ac5-47dd-8289-be1c515bd644', '9cae5902-32e0-4850-affc-dfc03abfe79c', 'Alcohol-related liver disease and alcohol use disorder are distinct, separately classified conditions in ICD-10-CM (unlike some other conditions with a true single combination code) — both should be coded when clinically relevant, reflecting accurate reporting of the full clinical picture rather than only one or the other.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('d91f9724-4b58-4575-b969-d01be155191c', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 37, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for a liver biopsy performed via percutaneous needle technique for diagnostic evaluation of suspected cirrhosis?');
VALUES ('d91f9724-4b58-4575-b969-d01be155191c', '53d4167c-27c3-40ff-b6d2-10ec62c77bdc', 'A percutaneous needle liver biopsy removes a small PORTION of tissue for diagnostic purposes — mapping to Excision (not Resection, which would require the entire organ) with the percutaneous approach and diagnostic qualifier, reinforcing the biopsy-coding principle already established for GI endoscopic biopsies.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('ff6cbbf3-f9f4-462d-a864-6d2a31b991c1', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 38, 'Which of the following best describes correct sequencing when a patient is admitted for symptomatic anemia requiring transfusion, later found to be due to an occult GI bleed from a duodenal ulcer identified on EGD during the same stay?');
VALUES ('ff6cbbf3-f9f4-462d-a864-6d2a31b991c1', '36772308-fa45-45e3-a00a-13302a33f01b', 'This directly applies UHDDS''s ''established AFTER STUDY'' principle — the initial presenting problem (anemia) needn''t be the final principal diagnosis once workup identifies the actual underlying condition (the bleeding ulcer) chiefly responsible for the admission, a frequently tested application of this core UHDDS concept.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('71372ce3-9c62-49d5-9860-531f7664c6b5', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 39, 'Which of the following best describes correct ICD-10-CM coding when a patient has documented ''acute kidney injury on chronic kidney disease'' (AKI on CKD)?');
VALUES ('71372ce3-9c62-49d5-9860-531f7664c6b5', 'c30d0cdd-1912-41e1-ab2c-1c4574b90538', 'Unlike heart failure or respiratory failure (which DO have acute-on-chronic combination codes), AKI and CKD remain SEPARATELY classified conditions in ICD-10-CM requiring both to be coded individually — an important contrast illustrating that the acute-on-chronic combination-code pattern doesn''t apply universally across all conditions, and coders must know which specific conditions do and don''t have this structure.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('ca665edd-f34c-4de2-950e-e0aee20bf6c1', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 40, 'Which of the following best describes correct ICD-10-CM coding when a patient has both cirrhosis AND hepatocellular carcinoma (a malignancy arising from the cirrhotic liver) documented?');
VALUES ('ca665edd-f34c-4de2-950e-e0aee20bf6c1', '9db139f4-1924-4b00-8ac2-689f05097613', 'Hepatocellular carcinoma and the underlying cirrhosis are distinct, separately classified conditions — both coded when clinically relevant, with sequencing (which is principal) determined by the actual reason for the specific encounter being coded, per UHDDS.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

-- ── Section: Domain 2 — Coding Documentation (20 questions) — Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA's CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance. ──

VALUES ('96019de2-dfde-4ead-ad12-d4f151ec1e69', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 41, 'A nephrology consult note documents ''ESRD on HD'' (hemodialysis) with no further detail on access type (AV fistula, graft, or catheter) anywhere in the current encounter''s documentation, though a prior encounter''s note mentions a fistula. Correct handling is best described as:');
VALUES ('96019de2-dfde-4ead-ad12-d4f151ec1e69', 'a543b7c4-db09-4f51-b659-d3a70eeb5888', 'Dialysis access type can genuinely change over time — assuming continuity from a prior encounter without current verification risks inaccuracy if a conversion has occurred; current documentation or a query (when access-type-specific coding is actually relevant) is the correct approach.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

VALUES ('4b1d7124-bdf7-48e9-a9de-c9f5af5fd54a', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 42, 'An endoscopy report documents ''multiple polyps removed throughout the colon'' without specifying the technique (snare, hot biopsy forceps, cold forceps) used for each individual polyp. From a documentation-completeness standpoint for accurate ICD-10-PCS root operation coding, this represents:');
VALUES ('4b1d7124-bdf7-48e9-a9de-c9f5af5fd54a', '6ede341d-31fa-4a12-bfbc-d989a6bc4491', 'Since technique determines root operation (Excision vs. Destruction), a report that only says ''removed'' without technique detail creates a genuine specificity gap — the full report should be reviewed for technique-specific detail per site, with a query if genuinely unresolved.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

VALUES ('c9298060-3cba-4d41-b412-b20ed0f24e15', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 43, 'A ventilator flow sheet shows intermittent gaps in documented hourly ventilator settings during an inpatient stay, with the total duration unclear due to these documentation gaps. Which best reflects the appropriate approach to determining ventilator hours for coding?');
VALUES ('c9298060-3cba-4d41-b412-b20ed0f24e15', '12d9b242-14c1-4e24-89c0-d785fc4789e6', 'A gap in ONE documentation source (the flow sheet) doesn''t necessarily mean the underlying clinical fact (continuous ventilation) is actually in question — cross-referencing other legitimate documentation sources (RT notes, orders) is the appropriate first step before assuming the shortest duration or declaring the fact un-codeable.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

VALUES ('ca46e6cb-bdb3-4179-baca-13c69cf6c4cb', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 44, 'A wound care note documents a lower extremity ulcer''s depth and size but doesn''t specify the underlying etiology (venous stasis, arterial insufficiency, diabetic, pressure) anywhere in that specific note, while a separate vascular surgery consult from the same admission documents ''venous stasis disease with associated ulceration.'' The correct documentation-review approach is:');
VALUES ('ca46e6cb-bdb3-4179-baca-13c69cf6c4cb', '59768332-f35e-40ea-bda2-78d2398d2077', 'Etiology-establishing documentation often comes from a different specialist (here, vascular surgery) than the provider documenting wound characteristics (wound care) — reviewing across the full record for the same episode, not limiting review to a single note type, is the correct approach to finding already-available specificity.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

VALUES ('2428fbc1-7bd8-4616-94ca-33008b5fa578', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 45, 'Which of the following best describes an appropriate documentation-integrity practice when a patient''s GI endoscopy pathology report (finalized several days after the procedure) reveals a finding (e.g., dysplasia) not known at the time the original procedure note was written?');
VALUES ('2428fbc1-7bd8-4616-94ca-33008b5fa578', '516bb67c-25ea-46d2-9ba2-512d4ccba20c', 'Final pathology results are a legitimate and often essential documentation source for accurate diagnosis coding (e.g., benign vs. dysplastic vs. malignant polyp characterization) — coding appropriately incorporates this information once available, rather than being frozen at the intraoperative note''s more limited information.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

VALUES ('d5cb09d0-0f02-4da6-9559-ec15781fb451', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 46, 'A GI endoscopy suite''s procedure log shows a colonoscopy was performed, but the actual dictated procedure note is not yet available in the chart at the time coding would otherwise occur, with only a brief nursing procedure note describing ''colonoscopy completed, polyps noted.'' The correct approach is:');
VALUES ('d5cb09d0-0f02-4da6-9559-ec15781fb451', 'cdefe0c4-4855-44f5-acec-992a5bbb0dc8', 'A brief nursing note doesn''t supply the technical detail needed for accurate procedure coding — the correct approach is holding coding for the physician''s own complete documentation (which is likely simply pending in normal workflow) rather than coding from inadequate documentation or immediately querying before checking whether the note is simply not yet finalized.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

VALUES ('a1dc2c49-2a0d-48c7-8894-257d22cf251f', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 47, 'A patient''s chart shows a ''ventilator weaning'' note indicating successful extubation, but no clear documentation anywhere establishing the exact date/time mechanical ventilation was initially started. Which best reflects the appropriate documentation-review approach for calculating ventilator duration?');
VALUES ('a1dc2c49-2a0d-48c7-8894-257d22cf251f', '406b72d7-b1b6-40e6-b4a8-1f178239dbcb', 'Ventilator initiation timing is often documented in a DIFFERENT part of the record (anesthesia records, ED notes, ICU admission documentation) than the weaning/extubation note — a thorough review across these sources, rather than assuming the information doesn''t exist, is the appropriate first step.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

VALUES ('c744f7e2-2f03-4a2e-8406-e3094be84a19', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 48, 'Which of the following best describes an appropriate documentation-integrity practice for verifying that a coded ''therapeutic'' (rather than diagnostic) qualifier for a drainage procedure (e.g., thoracentesis, paracentesis) is accurate?');
VALUES ('c744f7e2-2f03-4a2e-8406-e3094be84a19', 'a2fce5bc-a864-496d-a44d-05df576d6a7e', 'The diagnostic-versus-therapeutic qualifier distinction depends on the DOCUMENTED PURPOSE of the fluid removal, not an assumption based on the mere fact that drainage occurred — reviewing the procedure note/indication for the physician''s stated purpose is the correct verification approach.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

VALUES ('b6060d3a-9f42-4de4-9e3e-06ff398ca662', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 49, 'A patient''s EMTALA-relevant ED chart shows a medical screening examination note, but no clear documentation of the specific time the screening exam began relative to the patient''s arrival/triage time. Which best reflects a reasonable HIM documentation-completeness observation here (recognizing this is primarily an ED-operations/compliance matter, not itself a diagnosis-coding issue)?');
VALUES ('b6060d3a-9f42-4de4-9e3e-06ff398ca662', '117423cf-3df4-4115-9dab-fd4081f0a472', 'While not itself a diagnosis-coding accuracy issue, EMTALA-relevant timing documentation completeness is a genuine documentation-integrity concern worth recognizing/flagging through appropriate channels — connecting general documentation-completeness principles to this specific regulatory-compliance context.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

VALUES ('34700deb-c807-47e9-9ebc-9ea538eb3fc7', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 50, 'Which of the following best describes an appropriate documentation practice for a facility undergoing an accreditation survey, specifically regarding how HIM staff should respond if a surveyor requests to review a sample of health records?');
VALUES ('34700deb-c807-47e9-9ebc-9ea538eb3fc7', '44aa9951-84e6-4799-b820-7eabb6ca7056', 'Having an established, trained-on policy for accreditation survey record-review requests (rather than improvised, ad hoc handling) reflects sound HIM operational readiness — reinforcing the earlier point about continuous survey-readiness practice rather than crisis-mode preparation.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

VALUES ('c307b559-58ef-4d02-af5a-e336b59a453e', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 51, 'A telehealth visit note is notably briefer than the physician''s typical in-person visit documentation for a similar presenting problem, raising a question about whether it adequately supports the E/M level or diagnosis specificity being reported. Which best reflects the appropriate approach?');
VALUES ('c307b559-58ef-4d02-af5a-e336b59a453e', '78757ed5-8e4d-4ce5-8cad-8413c74468bc', 'This reinforces (for a third time in this content, across different domains) that documentation-sufficiency standards apply consistently regardless of visit modality — a brief telehealth note not supporting what''s being reported is treated the same as an inadequately-documented in-person visit, not held to an automatically lower bar.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

VALUES ('aa4a5687-4212-439f-bf05-37577ed4bfe4', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 52, 'Which best describes an appropriate documentation-integrity practice when a remote patient monitoring device transmits a clinically abnormal reading (e.g., a very high home blood pressure reading) directly into the EHR, with no clinician note anywhere addressing or acting on that specific reading?');
VALUES ('aa4a5687-4212-439f-bf05-37577ed4bfe4', '07bf3c37-749a-45f6-b10d-355427b89d56', 'An abnormal RPM reading with no clinician follow-up documentation is a genuine dual-purpose concern (patient safety and documentation/coding accuracy) — worth flagging rather than either ignoring it or automatically treating raw abnormal device data as an established diagnosis without clinical review.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

VALUES ('dad6caca-eb01-4902-930e-0d7f498f56aa', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 53, 'A patient''s chart includes both an EGD report describing ''multiple gastric ulcers'' and a separate pathology report on biopsied tissue from ''one of the ulcers'' that reveals H. pylori infection. No note clarifies whether the H. pylori finding should be attributed to all documented ulcers or only the specific biopsied one. Correct handling is best described as:');
VALUES ('dad6caca-eb01-4902-930e-0d7f498f56aa', '188d484e-be67-4001-98c0-4bfcc2c6312a', 'Extrapolating a single biopsy''s finding to multiple undocumented/unbiopsied sites is a coder inference the physician''s own documentation (or a query) should resolve, not something the coder should independently determine — a genuine, specific-to-this-scenario ambiguity worth recognizing rather than resolving via assumption in either direction.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

VALUES ('18e5a792-e79f-4cbc-a18f-d28723bae903', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 54, 'Which of the following best describes an appropriate documentation-integrity practice for verifying donor-source qualifier accuracy (e.g., allogeneic-related vs. allogeneic-unrelated) for a bone marrow or kidney transplant, when the transplant coordinator''s records and the surgeon''s operative note appear to use slightly different terminology for the same donor relationship?');
VALUES ('18e5a792-e79f-4cbc-a18f-d28723bae903', 'e99d65a6-ff26-44b9-bbab-4b74706f917a', 'Different documentation sources sometimes use different terminology to describe the SAME underlying clinical fact — the appropriate first step is confirming whether the sources actually conflict on the underlying fact (donor relationship) or are just using different terminology for the same thing, before concluding a genuine conflict exists requiring a query.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

VALUES ('c486e1ea-6093-411d-a499-086776460d61', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 55, 'Which of the following best describes an appropriate documentation-integrity consideration for a facility relying on interpreter services during a patient encounter, as it relates to the accuracy of documented history/symptoms used for subsequent coding?');
VALUES ('c486e1ea-6093-411d-a499-086776460d61', 'f4b012f3-1958-4ff7-8f24-629f3d6cfc75', 'Interpreter-assisted encounters don''t require a fundamentally different coding-documentation-review approach — the same diligence in ensuring documentation genuinely reflects the clinical encounter applies, with the interpreter''s involvement itself being relevant documentation for quality/compliance purposes rather than a barrier to normal coding review.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

VALUES ('98236ac2-cc6f-4572-bb05-0c9f2758598f', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 56, 'Which best describes an appropriate documentation-review practice when a patient''s chart contains both a current encounter''s clinical documentation AND an automatically-generated ''patient-reported outcomes'' questionnaire (e.g., a depression screening score) completed via a patient portal before the visit, with no physician note addressing or incorporating the questionnaire result?');
VALUES ('98236ac2-cc6f-4572-bb05-0c9f2758598f', '0f5a7bcb-9cc5-4f22-94f2-0b1d4b3dfb0a', 'This extends the same ''objective data requires clinical interpretation before supporting a diagnosis'' principle (already applied to lab values, RPM data, and genetic test results) to patient-reported outcome screening tools — valuable clinical input, but not itself a substitute for physician review/correlation.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

VALUES ('a0739e2d-3883-4661-8f75-e92d93949f25', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 57, 'A patient''s chart shows conflicting documentation about whether a GI bleed was ''upper'' or ''lower'' in origin — the ED note suggests upper GI bleed based on presentation, while the actual EGD (which was normal) and subsequent colonoscopy identified a bleeding source in the colon. Correct handling of this apparent conflict is best described as:');
VALUES ('a0739e2d-3883-4661-8f75-e92d93949f25', '6d86dbb0-dd79-4c1f-9053-384273ce2699', 'This distinguishes a genuine unresolved documentation CONFLICT from the normal, expected diagnostic process where an initial impression gets refined/corrected by subsequent test results — the more definitive, later diagnostic finding (confirmed lower GI source) appropriately governs coding, not an earlier, superseded clinical impression, and this doesn''t require a query when the workup itself clearly resolves the question.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

VALUES ('9eef25ef-a8d2-46cd-8a1f-85d1aab63f58', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 58, 'Which of the following best describes an appropriate documentation-integrity consideration when using computer-assisted coding (CAC) suggestions for a complex GI procedure note involving multiple techniques (e.g., biopsy via forceps at one site, snare polypectomy at another, and hot biopsy destruction at a third, all within one colonoscopy)?');
VALUES ('9eef25ef-a8d2-46cd-8a1f-85d1aab63f58', '7dc570f2-a128-4189-9d24-8fe0db81a927', 'This connects the earlier general CAC-verification-responsibility principle (Domain 5) to a concrete, complex multi-technique GI procedure scenario — CAC assistance doesn''t reduce the need for careful verification when a procedure genuinely involves multiple distinct techniques/root operations at different sites.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

VALUES ('070b844f-2ae6-4760-805d-a4e0ebb47405', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 59, 'Which of the following best describes an appropriate documentation practice for capturing whether a TIPS procedure (transjugular intrahepatic portosystemic shunt) used a covered or bare (uncovered) stent, given that this detail may affect device-value specificity?');
VALUES ('070b844f-2ae6-4760-805d-a4e0ebb47405', '0a1ed6b3-785a-46f2-a2ad-830b44e9bec9', 'This reapplies the device-specificity documentation principle (already established multiple times for cardiac stents and other devices) to TIPS procedures specifically — a recurring theme showing the same underlying documentation-verification principle applies across many different device-related procedures throughout this content.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

VALUES ('332c8964-a9d3-4de1-ac01-dae32cc650e5', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 60, 'A patient''s chart shows a nephrology progress note stating ''continue current dialysis regimen'' with the actual dialysis modality (hemodialysis vs. peritoneal dialysis) never explicitly restated in that note, though a much earlier note (from months prior) documented hemodialysis. Which best reflects the appropriate documentation-review approach for coding the CURRENT encounter''s dialysis-related procedures?');
VALUES ('332c8964-a9d3-4de1-ac01-dae32cc650e5', '7d4d2e99-7dd6-4778-bb70-bd6d3b903d7b', 'This reinforces the earlier dialysis-modality-can-change principle — current flow sheet/procedure documentation (not an old note or a vague ''continue regimen'' phrase) should establish which modality is actually in current use for accurate procedure coding of the current encounter.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

-- ── Section: Domain 3 — Provider Queries (10 questions) — Domain 3 (Provider Queries) — authored from AHIMA's CCS Exam Content Outline and AHIMA/ACDIS query-compliance guidance (non-leading query principles). ──

VALUES ('d8b6f400-99ca-431d-a50e-7fdfe86ece61', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 61, 'Which of the following best describes an appropriate, non-leading query to clarify dialysis access type when current documentation doesn''t specify it and access-type-specific coding is needed?');
VALUES ('d8b6f400-99ca-431d-a50e-7fdfe86ece61', 'c41abdb5-84d5-4bf3-9489-6c384f890525', 'This applies the standard non-leading, multi-option query construction to a nephrology-specific documentation gap — presenting genuine options rather than presuming one specific answer.', 'Domain 3 (Provider Queries) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and AHIMA/ACDIS query-compliance guidance (non-leading query principles).');

VALUES ('6c8010e2-f370-44a7-8f26-227ff4b8bf73', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 62, 'Which best describes an appropriate query approach for clarifying the technique used to remove a colon polyp when the operative report is ambiguous (e.g., states ''polyp removed'' without specifying snare, hot biopsy, or cold forceps technique)?');
VALUES ('6c8010e2-f370-44a7-8f26-227ff4b8bf73', '7c3ec920-7985-4cb2-958e-0e6381b6d278', 'Removal technique is a specific, physician-knowable fact directly affecting code selection — an appropriate, straightforward non-leading query subject, not something to assume by default or dismiss as beneath query-worthy significance.', 'Domain 3 (Provider Queries) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and AHIMA/ACDIS query-compliance guidance (non-leading query principles).');

VALUES ('a0d067f4-9021-4b81-b9de-d448a092a779', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 63, 'Which of the following best describes an appropriate query practice when total ventilator duration is genuinely unclear after reviewing all available documentation sources (flow sheets, RT notes, physician orders)?');
VALUES ('a0d067f4-9021-4b81-b9de-d448a092a779', '48d3c4b1-e946-45b3-bc43-565ea11ec5f9', 'When documentation review alone doesn''t resolve a genuine factual question (actual ventilator duration), a query to appropriately knowledgeable staff (physician or respiratory therapy) is a reasonable path — this is a legitimate factual clarification, not an administrative-only matter beneath query practice.', 'Domain 3 (Provider Queries) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and AHIMA/ACDIS query-compliance guidance (non-leading query principles).');

VALUES ('db842ca4-d512-4691-b785-1779ea65293d', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 64, 'Which of the following best describes an appropriate, non-leading query to clarify whether a colon polyp removal was performed via snare, hot biopsy forceps, or cold forceps, when the operative note doesn''t specify?');
VALUES ('db842ca4-d512-4691-b785-1779ea65293d', 'f3742e81-6840-44ec-b7f8-497b960cbf54', 'This applies standard non-leading, multi-option query construction to a GI-procedure-specific technique ambiguity — presenting genuine options rather than presuming the answer.', 'Domain 3 (Provider Queries) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and AHIMA/ACDIS query-compliance guidance (non-leading query principles).');

VALUES ('d2e5a6fe-edd1-421d-b8f7-67d21f9e1037', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 65, 'Which best describes an appropriate query approach for clarifying donor-source relationship (allogeneic-related vs. allogeneic-unrelated vs. autologous) for a transplant procedure when documentation is ambiguous?');
VALUES ('d2e5a6fe-edd1-421d-b8f7-67d21f9e1037', '54407cbd-b4b7-44bb-87cb-ed93a0e4461e', 'Donor-source relationship is a specific, transplant-team-knowable fact directly affecting code selection — an appropriate non-leading query subject, consistent with the device/qualifier-specificity query principle already established for other scenarios.', 'Domain 3 (Provider Queries) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and AHIMA/ACDIS query-compliance guidance (non-leading query principles).');

VALUES ('c2ba8f73-e4c5-4fe5-b20b-8ec527380305', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 66, 'Which of the following best describes an appropriate query practice when the diagnostic-versus-therapeutic intent of a drainage procedure (e.g., thoracentesis) is genuinely unclear from the documentation?');
VALUES ('c2ba8f73-e4c5-4fe5-b20b-8ec527380305', '0f97427d-139c-4a37-b43c-2c00ed3e55dd', 'A genuine ambiguity about documented procedural intent (diagnostic vs. therapeutic) is an appropriate, straightforward non-leading query subject — not something to default or assume without verification when genuinely unclear.', 'Domain 3 (Provider Queries) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and AHIMA/ACDIS query-compliance guidance (non-leading query principles).');

VALUES ('6b7fb775-b644-4272-9fdc-0b8e3b9986b2', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 67, 'Which of the following best describes an appropriate query approach when it''s unclear from documentation whether a GI bleed source finding (identified on a later diagnostic test) should supersede an earlier clinical impression, in a case where the record doesn''t clearly resolve this on its own?');
VALUES ('6b7fb775-b644-4272-9fdc-0b8e3b9986b2', 'cc8ee0e7-9386-41ef-a204-bf3de8a53a8e', 'Even though many such sequences resolve themselves through the normal diagnostic-refinement process (as covered in Domain 2), a genuinely ambiguous case not resolved by the record itself still warrants an open, non-presumptive query about the physician''s current, final clinical determination.', 'Domain 3 (Provider Queries) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and AHIMA/ACDIS query-compliance guidance (non-leading query principles).');

VALUES ('7fb4bb70-f2e0-4fbe-9644-e9a5d622334d', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 68, 'Which of the following best describes an appropriate compliance consideration when constructing a query about EMTALA-relevant documentation gaps (e.g., unclear timing of a medical screening exam)?');
VALUES ('7fb4bb70-f2e0-4fbe-9644-e9a5d622334d', 'bc2757bb-998e-4ca8-a117-d891e57947b8', 'A factually-framed request for documentation completion (asking what actually happened, not suggesting a predetermined answer) follows the same non-leading principles as clinical specificity queries — applied here to a regulatory-documentation-completeness context rather than a diagnosis/procedure specificity context.', 'Domain 3 (Provider Queries) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and AHIMA/ACDIS query-compliance guidance (non-leading query principles).');

VALUES ('388eb91d-115d-4b15-9f8b-d214de6892cf', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 69, 'Which of the following best describes an appropriate query practice when a telehealth visit''s documentation doesn''t clearly establish whether time or MDM was used as the basis for the reported E/M level?');
VALUES ('388eb91d-115d-4b15-9f8b-d214de6892cf', '734043c9-5552-4ee4-9037-3da0504a6e0b', 'This connects the earlier E/M time-vs-MDM leveling discussion to a documentation-clarification/query scenario — the coder needs clarity on which basis is actually being used and supported, rather than defaulting to whichever produces a higher code level without genuine documentation support.', 'Domain 3 (Provider Queries) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and AHIMA/ACDIS query-compliance guidance (non-leading query principles).');

VALUES ('72f833b2-9898-4402-afa8-adad6057cf46', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 70, 'Which best describes the appropriate scope of a compliant query regarding remote patient monitoring data that appears clinically significant but is unaddressed in any physician note?');
VALUES ('72f833b2-9898-4402-afa8-adad6057cf46', 'c7d45896-e00e-4d98-b1ec-e9d616cf92b1', 'This applies standard non-leading query construction to an RPM-data scenario — presenting the objective finding and asking for genuine clinical judgment (including the possibility the finding isn''t significant), not presuming a specific diagnostic conclusion.', 'Domain 3 (Provider Queries) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and AHIMA/ACDIS query-compliance guidance (non-leading query principles).');

-- ── Section: Domain 4 — Regulatory Compliance (20 questions) — Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA's CCS Exam Content Outline, EMTALA basics, and accreditation (Joint Commission-type) survey-readiness concepts. ──

VALUES ('b4629d55-6d37-47e3-9218-c39657657095', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 71, 'The Emergency Medical Treatment and Labor Act (EMTALA) generally requires which of the following of Medicare-participating hospitals with an emergency department?');
VALUES ('b4629d55-6d37-47e3-9218-c39657657095', 'bc11a0a3-ee30-40d9-97ef-10c9c20b7f55', 'EMTALA''s core requirements — a medical screening exam and stabilization/appropriate transfer for an emergency medical condition, regardless of ability to pay — apply broadly to Medicare-participating hospitals with EDs, regardless of arrival method or state-level opt-in (it''s a federal requirement).', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, EMTALA basics, and accreditation (Joint Commission-type) survey-readiness concepts.');

VALUES ('2549dd72-838f-4738-b30e-c7c35e2a79c1', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 72, 'Which of the following best describes the HIM/coding-relevant documentation connection to EMTALA compliance, even though EMTALA is primarily a clinical/administrative ED-operations law?');
VALUES ('2549dd72-838f-4738-b30e-c7c35e2a79c1', '4ee577b3-3553-40d7-a2fc-92540d599217', 'EMTALA documentation (medical screening exam, transfer determinations) both demonstrates regulatory compliance and supports accurate coding of the ED encounter itself — another example of documentation-completeness principles intersecting with a specific regulatory framework.', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, EMTALA basics, and accreditation (Joint Commission-type) survey-readiness concepts.');

VALUES ('e2d54f10-0475-477c-a8b4-b1a76ba4d9bd', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 73, 'Which of the following best describes a ''transfer'' under EMTALA, in the context of a patient with an unstabilized emergency medical condition being moved to another facility?');
VALUES ('e2d54f10-0475-477c-a8b4-b1a76ba4d9bd', 'b836788c-ee9f-4c7a-a3fd-02f5b0cdf7ad', 'EMTALA''s ''appropriate transfer'' requirements for an unstabilized patient involve specific conditions (physician certification, receiving facility acceptance, appropriate transport support) — not automatically satisfied by any transfer regardless of process, and applicable regardless of state lines or transport method.', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, EMTALA basics, and accreditation (Joint Commission-type) survey-readiness concepts.');

VALUES ('6ea90804-b8d7-468b-94b1-58a977e5f696', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 74, 'Which of the following best describes the general purpose of hospital accreditation surveys (e.g., by The Joint Commission or another CMS-approved accrediting organization) as they relate to HIM/coding department practices?');
VALUES ('6ea90804-b8d7-468b-94b1-58a977e5f696', 'c0502687-5ff3-424f-ad69-d0c62f8a12ff', 'Accreditation (often satisfying CMS ''deemed status'' requirements for Medicare/Medicaid participation) typically includes review of health record documentation practices and HIM policies — a genuine, recurring survey-readiness responsibility touching HIM/coding operations, not a purely clinical-nursing-focused review.', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, EMTALA basics, and accreditation (Joint Commission-type) survey-readiness concepts.');

VALUES ('fe834a2d-40bf-4ddb-b4f3-a6614728d7ac', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 75, 'Which of the following best describes a reasonable HIM department practice to support ongoing accreditation survey-readiness (rather than only preparing intensively right before a scheduled survey)?');
VALUES ('fe834a2d-40bf-4ddb-b4f3-a6614728d7ac', '6a629d8f-c2f1-4728-bace-e905812bdd35', 'Given that many accrediting bodies use unannounced survey visits, and that accreditation standards do evolve, continuous/ongoing compliance practice (not periodic crisis-mode preparation) is the more sustainable, lower-risk, and actually-expected approach to survey readiness.', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, EMTALA basics, and accreditation (Joint Commission-type) survey-readiness concepts.');

VALUES ('b25f937b-c346-4ff3-bb27-f92d845f2c73', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 76, 'Which of the following best describes a compliance-relevant reason for HIM leadership to track and trend health record delinquency rates (e.g., incomplete/unsigned records beyond a facility''s policy timeframe)?');
VALUES ('b25f937b-c346-4ff3-bb27-f92d845f2c73', 'a92d8d11-2785-472a-b271-8c8bb75a038a', 'Record delinquency is a recognized accreditation/compliance risk area with real downstream effects on coding accuracy and documentation completeness — proactive HIM tracking/trending (not waiting for a survey to surface the issue) is standard, expected practice.', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, EMTALA basics, and accreditation (Joint Commission-type) survey-readiness concepts.');

VALUES ('2de30ac4-cacd-43f4-b77a-f07bc86b5e52', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 77, 'Which of the following best describes the general concept of an ''on-call'' physician''s EMTALA obligations for a hospital with an emergency department?');
VALUES ('2de30ac4-cacd-43f4-b77a-f07bc86b5e52', 'a5036183-e048-475e-a741-2e1dfd39e521', 'EMTALA''s on-call requirements extend the law''s reach beyond just the ED physician to specialists needed for stabilization — a hospital''s on-call list and physicians'' responsiveness are both part of the overall EMTALA compliance picture.', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, EMTALA basics, and accreditation (Joint Commission-type) survey-readiness concepts.');

VALUES ('1eabd741-012d-45de-b2e6-ac21156c8122', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 78, 'Which of the following best describes a ''certification of transfer'' requirement under EMTALA for an unstabilized patient being transferred to another facility?');
VALUES ('1eabd741-012d-45de-b2e6-ac21156c8122', '1142ed49-552a-4afb-9e2f-c5d400fbac87', 'Physician certification that transfer benefits outweigh risks is a specific, required documented element of an appropriate EMTALA transfer for an unstabilized patient — not a universal ''no certification ever needed'' situation, nor limited to specific times or patient populations.', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, EMTALA basics, and accreditation (Joint Commission-type) survey-readiness concepts.');

VALUES ('c8ab4306-632c-402d-8c9f-b2a60ddef313', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 79, 'Which of the following best describes a facility''s obligation regarding EMTALA compliance for a patient presenting to a hospital-owned, off-campus facility that meets the definition of a ''dedicated emergency department''?');
VALUES ('c8ab4306-632c-402d-8c9f-b2a60ddef313', '4f64edac-b5fd-4196-9936-d5bb3d2b929f', 'EMTALA''s reach can extend to qualifying off-campus, hospital-owned dedicated emergency departments — a facility operating multiple sites needs to understand which specific locations meet the regulatory definition triggering these obligations, not assume EMTALA is limited to only the main on-campus ED.', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, EMTALA basics, and accreditation (Joint Commission-type) survey-readiness concepts.');

VALUES ('417168ab-cf4e-4e32-8af8-a80cab3d5b68', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 80, 'Which of the following best describes the general concept of a ''deemed status'' hospital under CMS regulations?');
VALUES ('417168ab-cf4e-4e32-8af8-a80cab3d5b68', 'a7d12868-82f5-426d-8171-7fb665ee0522', 'Deemed status reflects the practical relationship between private accreditation (Joint Commission-type surveys) and CMS Conditions of Participation compliance — accreditation streamlines but doesn''t entirely eliminate CMS''s oversight authority (complaint surveys, validation surveys can still occur).', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, EMTALA basics, and accreditation (Joint Commission-type) survey-readiness concepts.');

VALUES ('f2aa6713-a34f-4955-811d-6e4bae0c36c2', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 81, 'Which of the following best describes a reasonable compliance-program response when an accreditation survey identifies a finding related to HIM documentation practices (e.g., a pattern of delinquent or incomplete records)?');
VALUES ('f2aa6713-a34f-4955-811d-6e4bae0c36c2', 'd50e3e45-fb8f-42d2-8ba0-072ec3a3112b', 'This connects the earlier general corrective-action-program principles to accreditation-specific findings — unaddressed findings carry real consequences (accreditation/deemed status, and by extension CMS participation), making genuine root-cause corrective action the appropriate response, not automatic disputation or inaction.', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, EMTALA basics, and accreditation (Joint Commission-type) survey-readiness concepts.');

VALUES ('48220724-5224-4671-9aba-632e1f6719cd', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 82, 'Which of the following best describes a reasonable HIM-department contribution to a facility''s overall EMTALA compliance program, distinct from the ED''s own clinical/operational EMTALA responsibilities?');
VALUES ('48220724-5224-4671-9aba-632e1f6719cd', '922a05f7-c283-4483-b9de-9b4713142815', 'HIM''s EMTALA-adjacent role centers on documentation completeness/retention/retrievability supporting compliance demonstration — a distinct but complementary role to the ED''s own clinical/operational EMTALA responsibilities, not overlapping billing-only or zero-role extremes.', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, EMTALA basics, and accreditation (Joint Commission-type) survey-readiness concepts.');

VALUES ('0163545c-ed9f-414b-95db-4a857d5ee3de', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 83, 'Which of the following best describes a reasonable compliance consideration for a facility using telehealth to extend on-call specialist coverage for EMTALA purposes (e.g., a telehealth-based neurology consult for a stroke patient in a rural ED)?');
VALUES ('0163545c-ed9f-414b-95db-4a857d5ee3de', '6475f354-0a17-41e0-994f-2ede11d7de24', 'This connects the telehealth technology discussion (Domain 5) to EMTALA on-call coverage concepts (Domain 4) — telehealth-based specialist coverage can be a legitimate, increasingly common way to help meet coverage needs, subject to meeting the underlying substantive requirements (timely, adequate evaluation), not an automatic disqualification or an entirely unregulated arrangement.', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, EMTALA basics, and accreditation (Joint Commission-type) survey-readiness concepts.');

VALUES ('c8b8583e-8924-46b5-af6e-b465d8c4e992', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 84, 'Which of the following best describes a compliance-relevant reason for a facility to periodically review whether its transplant program''s donor-source documentation practices meet both clinical/regulatory (e.g., organ procurement organization requirements) and coding-accuracy needs simultaneously?');
VALUES ('c8b8583e-8924-46b5-af6e-b465d8c4e992', '9bf96bc8-f77c-475f-9ece-f49f77138d26', 'Recognizing that the SAME donor-source documentation serves multiple purposes (regulatory/registry reporting AND coding specificity) supports a more integrated, efficient documentation-review approach — reducing the risk that a gap relevant to one purpose goes unnoticed because the documentation happens to satisfy a different purpose''s requirements.', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, EMTALA basics, and accreditation (Joint Commission-type) survey-readiness concepts.');

VALUES ('b66f0cdc-a96c-4d51-8e3c-642dc8f1d00c', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 85, 'Which of the following best describes a compliance-relevant reason a facility might specifically audit ventilator-hour documentation and coding accuracy, given its direct DRG/reimbursement impact?');
VALUES ('b66f0cdc-a96c-4d51-8e3c-642dc8f1d00c', '0f3d50d3-5b96-4e86-a4e2-bfdcee10248b', 'This applies the earlier ''targeted/focused audit for a known higher-risk, documentation-complex, financially-significant area'' rationale (already established for POA/HAC auditing) to ventilator-hour coding specifically — a reasonable, similarly-justified focused compliance activity.', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, EMTALA basics, and accreditation (Joint Commission-type) survey-readiness concepts.');

VALUES ('ee8a6cb0-9400-4be3-bdf3-d15dc62b2b9b', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 86, 'Which of the following best describes a compliance-relevant reason for coordinating HIM/coding compliance monitoring with a facility''s broader quality/patient-safety committee structure (e.g., regarding PSI/HAC rates, EMTALA compliance, and accreditation findings)?');
VALUES ('ee8a6cb0-9400-4be3-bdf3-d15dc62b2b9b', 'b4a59171-f464-4e3f-9c51-eadd48ca9848', 'This is a reasonable synthesis point: because many Domain 4 topics have BOTH a coding-accuracy dimension and a genuine clinical quality/safety dimension, coordinated (not siloed) monitoring across HIM/coding compliance and quality/safety committee functions supports more complete issue identification and response.', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, EMTALA basics, and accreditation (Joint Commission-type) survey-readiness concepts.');

VALUES ('4a61988c-14bb-4f8c-bec3-30ecfe7db07a', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 87, 'Which of the following best describes a compliance-relevant reason to specifically track query response patterns for RPM-data-triggered queries (e.g., how often a physician determines an RPM finding is ''not clinically significant'' versus warranting a new diagnosis)?');
VALUES ('4a61988c-14bb-4f8c-bec3-30ecfe7db07a', '0499f059-4498-4e0d-837b-7a29701b13ee', 'This extends the general query-outcome-pattern-monitoring principle to RPM-specific queries — a forward-looking, reasonable compliance-monitoring application given RPM''s growing role in care and its potential intersection with HCC/risk-adjustment coding accuracy concerns already discussed.', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, EMTALA basics, and accreditation (Joint Commission-type) survey-readiness concepts.');

VALUES ('fd71b8da-2e80-476d-bb01-86a2927cf8f4', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 88, 'Which of the following best describes the compliance rationale for a facility maintaining a documented policy on how long telehealth visit recordings (if any are made) are retained, and who may access them?');
VALUES ('fd71b8da-2e80-476d-bb01-86a2927cf8f4', '4e220d76-15df-4c2f-96fe-025d246921d0', 'Telehealth recordings (where created) are a form of PHI raising their own retention/access-control considerations that warrant clear facility policy — an extension of general HIM records-management/compliance principles to a specific, relatively newer technology-driven data type.', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, EMTALA basics, and accreditation (Joint Commission-type) survey-readiness concepts.');

VALUES ('ed47ee09-7c98-4f69-a0b1-bbf1ec51f927', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 89, 'Which of the following best describes a compliance-relevant reason to periodically reassess whether a facility''s information-blocking exception practices (discussed earlier) appropriately account for newer data types like RPM data and telehealth visit records, as these become more prevalent?');
VALUES ('ed47ee09-7c98-4f69-a0b1-bbf1ec51f927', '0efed4c6-66d2-44c2-84c3-f1ae9e315c79', 'This reinforces the earlier ''ongoing monitoring, not one-time achievement'' principle for information-blocking compliance, applied specifically to the evolving data landscape (RPM, telehealth) discussed throughout this paper''s Domain 5 content — policies should evolve as the underlying data types they govern evolve.', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, EMTALA basics, and accreditation (Joint Commission-type) survey-readiness concepts.');

VALUES ('560ecb51-faea-4dbf-b12a-0c84a9c1e0c6', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 90, 'Which of the following best describes a compliance-relevant reason a facility''s overall EMTALA policy should be reviewed and updated periodically (rather than treated as a static, one-time document)?');
VALUES ('560ecb51-faea-4dbf-b12a-0c84a9c1e0c6', 'f89135ec-6c21-45ec-a1cc-c251415d54fe', 'This reinforces the ''ongoing monitoring, not one-time achievement'' compliance theme (already established for information-blocking and other regulatory areas) applied specifically to EMTALA policy — evolving guidance and evolving facility operations (new sites, telehealth arrangements) both argue for periodic, not one-time, policy review.', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, EMTALA basics, and accreditation (Joint Commission-type) survey-readiness concepts.');

-- ── Section: Domain 5 — Information Technologies (10 questions) — Domain 5 (Information Technologies) — authored from AHIMA's CCS Exam Content Outline covering telehealth technology standards and remote patient monitoring data integrity. ──

VALUES ('4de07d6f-1b7c-402a-a600-45abce445cad', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 91, 'Which of the following best describes a data-integrity consideration specific to remote patient monitoring (RPM) device data (e.g., a home blood glucose monitor or blood pressure cuff transmitting readings to the EHR) being used to support clinical documentation/coding?');
VALUES ('4de07d6f-1b7c-402a-a600-45abce445cad', '9b41a440-e2a9-4a49-b40b-eef81bead917', 'This extends the earlier ''raw test result requires clinical interpretation'' principle to remote patient monitoring device data specifically — valuable clinical input, but subject to its own data-quality risks (calibration, transmission, usage errors) and best incorporated via clinician review/interpretation rather than treated as an automatic, self-interpreting clinical determination.', 'Domain 5 (Information Technologies) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline covering telehealth technology standards and remote patient monitoring data integrity.');

VALUES ('9ca273a4-3285-4190-adc9-b917fc2904f2', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 92, 'Which of the following best describes a technology-standards consideration relevant to telehealth platforms used for behavioral health services subject to 42 CFR Part 2 protections?');
VALUES ('9ca273a4-3285-4190-adc9-b917fc2904f2', '0f323f13-15b7-4d0a-b2b8-fa6aff7b1e9b', 'This connects the earlier Part 2 discussion to concrete telehealth technology considerations — platform selection/configuration for Part 2-covered services should reflect appropriate security/privacy safeguards, not treat all communication tools as interchangeable regardless of the sensitivity of what''s being discussed.', 'Domain 5 (Information Technologies) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline covering telehealth technology standards and remote patient monitoring data integrity.');

VALUES ('b34a7b8a-5573-4562-a57c-6244530a780a', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 93, 'Which of the following best describes a reasonable HIM/compliance consideration when a telehealth encounter generates less structured documentation than a comparable in-person visit (e.g., a brief video visit note lacking the detail typically captured during an in-person physical exam)?');
VALUES ('b34a7b8a-5573-4562-a57c-6244530a780a', 'b535e6f4-d096-4507-ac89-3229af59ce09', 'This reinforces the earlier point (already made regarding HCC capture) that documentation-sufficiency standards don''t relax simply because an encounter occurred via telehealth — whatever is coded/billed for that encounter still needs to be supported by that encounter''s own actual documentation, regardless of modality.', 'Domain 5 (Information Technologies) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline covering telehealth technology standards and remote patient monitoring data integrity.');

VALUES ('ca565a47-ce7a-42c0-9cbe-52752d4dc47b', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 94, 'Which of the following best describes a reasonable HIM/IT governance question to ask when evaluating whether a telehealth platform adequately supports accurate coding/documentation workflows?');
VALUES ('ca565a47-ce7a-42c0-9cbe-52752d4dc47b', '4b836663-2b03-404a-92d6-c066c83e4b04', 'EHR integration (avoiding a disconnected, siloed telehealth system requiring manual reconciliation) is a practical, coding-workflow-relevant technology-governance consideration — connecting Domain 5''s technology-evaluation themes to real HIM/coding operational concerns.', 'Domain 5 (Information Technologies) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline covering telehealth technology standards and remote patient monitoring data integrity.');

VALUES ('8e4904ec-bd5f-4c5e-a09e-875ee6985909', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 95, 'Which of the following best describes a reasonable technology consideration for integrating RPM device data feeds into an EHR in a way that supports (rather than undermines) the clinical-review-before-coding principle already established?');
VALUES ('8e4904ec-bd5f-4c5e-a09e-875ee6985909', 'a43beb7b-33b8-473d-84cc-0038b9af7ed8', 'Thoughtful RPM-EHR integration design (routing significant findings to a clinician review queue) operationally supports the clinical-review-before-coding principle already established — a practical technology-design consideration connecting Domain 5''s technical content to Domain 1/2''s documentation-integrity principles.', 'Domain 5 (Information Technologies) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline covering telehealth technology standards and remote patient monitoring data integrity.');

VALUES ('9ed33764-84d4-4688-83db-46ba76d3eaf3', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 96, 'Which of the following best describes a reasonable vendor-evaluation question when selecting an RPM platform, given the data-integrity considerations already discussed (calibration, transmission errors, usage errors)?');
VALUES ('9ed33764-84d4-4688-83db-46ba76d3eaf3', '2be36bb9-a719-422b-8ed6-f682a17b6346', 'This connects the earlier RPM data-quality-risk discussion (calibration, transmission, usage errors) to a concrete vendor-evaluation practice — selecting platforms with data-validation features is a reasonable way to help mitigate those already-identified risks at the technology-selection stage.', 'Domain 5 (Information Technologies) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline covering telehealth technology standards and remote patient monitoring data integrity.');

VALUES ('fb6c8cea-a8a4-4805-86e5-93e305f8681e', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 97, 'Which of the following best describes a reasonable HIM/IT consideration for telehealth platforms specifically supporting EMTALA-related on-call specialist consultations (as discussed in Domain 4)?');
VALUES ('fb6c8cea-a8a4-4805-86e5-93e305f8681e', '7392fbc3-ffff-473c-8144-b4696801d3ae', 'Given the clinical stakes and compliance-documentation needs of EMTALA-related consultations specifically, platform reliability/documentation-capture capability is a particularly important technology consideration for this use case — connecting Domain 4''s EMTALA/on-call discussion to Domain 5''s technology-evaluation themes.', 'Domain 5 (Information Technologies) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline covering telehealth technology standards and remote patient monitoring data integrity.');

VALUES ('5e51c0cc-3f80-4065-839b-5facc977b679', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 98, 'Which of the following best describes a reasonable technology-governance practice for ensuring accreditation-survey-readiness specifically regarding electronic health record documentation completeness metrics (e.g., delinquency rates, discussed in Domain 4)?');
VALUES ('5e51c0cc-3f80-4065-839b-5facc977b679', '5a964e7d-f0aa-45a5-974e-9278adbe2cf3', 'This connects the earlier continuous-survey-readiness principle to a concrete technology practice — using EHR/HIM system reporting capabilities for ongoing metric tracking supports that continuous readiness goal more effectively than relying solely on periodic manual review.', 'Domain 5 (Information Technologies) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline covering telehealth technology standards and remote patient monitoring data integrity.');

VALUES ('c2de3ddf-73da-4336-9fea-e999b1ee353e', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 99, 'Which of the following best describes a reasonable way to think about the overall trajectory of HIM/coding technology competency, connecting encoder/CAC tools, interoperability standards, cybersecurity awareness, information-blocking/patient access, and now RPM/telehealth data integrity?');
VALUES ('c2de3ddf-73da-4336-9fea-e999b1ee353e', '7a95c2fa-2576-40e5-9616-70f0ebc561bf', 'This is a reasonable capstone synthesis: newer data sources and technologies don''t represent an entirely separate skill set, but rather extensions of the SAME underlying documentation-integrity, coding-accuracy, and compliance principles already established throughout this content — applied to an expanding range of data sources and technologies.', 'Domain 5 (Information Technologies) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline covering telehealth technology standards and remote patient monitoring data integrity.');

VALUES ('574f900f-fac7-4d94-928a-02e3d1a31d64', '906a82ef-afbb-474b-a8cc-ba6808b604bf', 100, 'Which of the following best summarizes why a well-rounded CCS-credentialed coder benefits from cross-domain awareness spanning coding knowledge (Domain 1), documentation integrity (Domain 2), query practice (Domain 3), regulatory compliance (Domain 4), and information technology (Domain 5), rather than treating these as entirely separate, unrelated competency areas?');
VALUES ('574f900f-fac7-4d94-928a-02e3d1a31d64', '6fbe6c2b-dbe0-47ae-bf35-88fcd91a6415', 'This is the paper''s capstone synthesis point: the recurring scenarios throughout this content (HCC coding, POA/HAC accuracy, device specificity, query construction) each genuinely span multiple domains simultaneously — reflecting AHIMA''s own exam content outline structure as a description of integrated real-world competency, not five isolated, unrelated skill silos.', 'Domain 5 (Information Technologies) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline covering telehealth technology standards and remote patient monitoring data integrity.');
